# Official docs : https://plantuml.com/zh/guide
# pdf download : https://pdf.plantuml.net/PlantUML_Language_Reference_Guide_zh.pdf

snippet plantUmlInit initialize plant uml
	\`\`\`plantuml
	@startuml
	
	title ${1:title}
	
	$0
	@enduml
	\`\`\`

# ```plantuml
# @startuml
# allowmixing
# title mixed diagram test
# 
# ' class diagram (class-diagram)
# class ClassDiagram { }
# ' deployment diagram (deployment-diagram)
# agent DeploymentAgent
# ' entity relationship diagram (ie-diagram)
# Entity01 }|..|| Entity02
# Entity03 }o..o| Entity04
# Entity05 ||--o{ Entity06
# Entity07 |o--|| Entity08
# ' json
# json XX {
#     "k": "v"
# }
# ' object
# object Object1
# object "Object number 2" as o2
# 
# @enduml
# ```
snippet plantUmlInitAllowmixing initialize plant uml with allowmixing set, letting a class diagram contain other diagram elements; only some diagrams can be mixed, supported: class, deployment, ie (entity relationship), json, object
	\`\`\`plantuml
	@startuml
	allowmixing
	title ${1:title}
	
	$0
	@enduml
	\`\`\`

# #Style
# ##Lines#Style
snippet plantUmlStyleLineOrtho line style: straight, default is curved
	skinparam linetype ortho

snippet plantUmlStyleLinePolyline line style: polyline, default is curved
	skinparam linetype polyline

# ##Auto numbering
snippet plantUmlStyleAutoNumber automatically add numbers in a flow diagram
	autonumber
	$0

# ##Direction
snippet plantUmlStyleDirectionLeftToRight lay out left to right, default is top to bottom
	left to right direction
	$0

snippet plantUmlStyleDirectionRightToLeft lay out right to left, default is top to bottom
	right to left direction
	$0

snippet plantUmlStyleDirectionTopToBottom lay out top to bottom, default is top to bottom
	top to bottom direction
	$0

snippet plantUmlStyleDirectionBottomToTop lay out bottom to top, default is top to bottom
	bottom to top direction
	$0

# #Notes
snippet plantUmlNote plant note, example : note {left|right|top|bottom|over} of Object #{Color} : {NoteContext}  1. Object : the annotated object 1.1 class diagram: for a class field use Object::field (fields need plantUmlNoteMulti); for overloaded methods wrap in '' quotes 'name(int length)' 1.2 some uml diagrams can link a note to two objects, separated by a comma : A,B 2. NoteContet : note content 3. Color: color code such as 000000 or blue
	note ${1:{left|right|top|bottom|over\}} of ${2:Object} : ${3:noteContet}
	$0

snippet plantUmlNoteMulti plant multi-line note, can annotate class fields, example : note {left|right|top|bottom|over} of Object #{Color}\n {NoteContext} \nend note ,1. Object : the annotated object 1.1 class diagram: for a class field use Object::field; for overloaded methods wrap in '' quotes 'name(int length)' 1.2 some uml diagrams can link a note to two objects, separated by a comma : A,B 2. NoteContet : note content 3. Color: color code such as 000000 or blue
	note ${1:{left|right|top|bottom|over\}} of ${2:Object}
	${3:noteContet}
	end note
	$0

snippet plantUmlNoteFloat floating note, treats the note as an object linked with a link, so a note can point at multiple objects : note '{NoteContext}' as {NoteName} \n {NoteName} {Link} {Object} 1. NoteContext: note content 2. NoteName : note name 3. Link: a link such as .. or --> for different styles 4. Object: the object the note points at
	note "${1:NoteContext}" as ${2:NoteName}
	$2 .. ${3:Object}
	$0

snippet plantUmlNoteLink note on a class diagram link (relationship); it must come after the link and annotates the previous link, note {left|right|top|bottom} on link #{Color} \n end note
	note ${1:{left|right|top|bottom|over\}}  on link
	$2
	end note
	$0

# #Class diagram
# Supported class diagram element types
# | Type           | Description        | Shape      |
# |----------------+--------------------+------------|
# | abstract       | abstract           | (A)        |
# | abstract class | abstract class     | (A)        |
# | annotation     | annotation         | (@)        |
# | circle         | circle             | ○          |
# | ()             | circle_short_form  | ○          |
# | class          | class              | (C)        |
# | diamond        | diamond            | ◇          |
# | <>             | diamond_short_form | ◇          |
# | entity         | entity             | (E) green  |
# | enum           | enum               | (E) orange |
# | exception      | exception          | (X)        |
# | interface      | interface          | (I)        |
# | metaclass      | metaclass          | (M)        |
# | protocol       | protocol           | (P)        |
# | stereotype     | stereotype         | (S) pink   |
# | struct         | struct             | (S) white  |

# Defining visibility
# | Char | Visibility      | Field icon | Method icon |
# |------+-----------------+------------+-------------|
# | -    | private         | □          | ◼           |
# | #    | protected       | ◇          | ◇           |
# | ~    | package private | △          | ▲           |
# | +    | public          | ○          | ●           |

snippet plantUmlClassInit plant uml class initialization, fields/methods are defined as standard java code
	class ${1:class_name} {
	$0
	}

# #Class diagram##Class relationships
# | Type                     | Symbol | Shape | Purpose                                            |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Generalization (is-a)    | --|>   | ──▷   | inheritance, example: A "label" --|> "label, B can |
# |                          |        |       | be (B,C) for two classes B,C" B : label , A        |
# |                          |        |       | extends B                                          |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Realization              | ..|>   | --▷   | a class implements an interface, example: A        |
# |                          |        |       | "label" ..|> "label, B can be (B,C) for two        |
# |                          |        |       | classes B,C" B : label , A implements B            |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Dependency               | ..>    | -->   | a weaker form of dependency, a method of one       |
# |                          |        |       | object uses another object, example: A "label" ..> |
# |                          |        |       | "label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C" B :    |
# |                          |        |       | label , a method of A uses B                       |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | One-way association      | -->    | ──>   | uses relationship, one object uses (holds as a     |
# |                          |        |       | global or class attribute) another object (e.g. a  |
# |                          |        |       | classroom has desks), example: A "label" -->       |
# |                          |        |       | "label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C" B :    |
# |                          |        |       | label , A has B as an attribute or global          |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Two-way association      | -->    | ──>   | mutual use, two objects use (hold as a global or   |
# |                          |        |       | class attribute) each other (e.g. a teacher has    |
# |                          |        |       | many students and each student has a teacher),     |
# |                          |        |       | example: A "label" -- "label, B can be (B,C) for   |
# |                          |        |       | two classes B,C" B : label , A has B as an         |
# |                          |        |       | attribute or global and B has A                    |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Aggregation (has-a)      | o--    | ◇ ──  | whole-part relationship (a special association),   |
# |                          |        |       | the part can exist independently of the whole      |
# |                          |        |       | (e.g. a company and its employees), example: A     |
# |                          |        |       | "label" o-- "label, B can be (B,C) for two classes |
# |                          |        |       | B,C" B : label, A has B as an attribute or global, |
# |                          |        |       | B aggregates into A                                |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|
# | Composition (contains-a) | *--    | ◆ ──  | containment (a special association, strong         |
# |                          |        |       | aggregation), the part cannot exist without the    |
# |                          |        |       | whole (e.g. a person and their brain), example: A  |
# |                          |        |       | "label" *-- "label, B can be (B,C) for two classes |
# |                          |        |       | B,C" B : label, A has B as an attribute or global, |
# |                          |        |       | B composes A                                       |
# |--------------------------+--------+-------+----------------------------------------------------|


#  Generalization (inheritance) (──▷ ):--|> example class1 "label" --|> "label" class2 : label
#  Realization (--▷ ):..|> example class1 "label" ..|> "label" class2 : label
#  Dependency (-->):..> example class1 "label" ..> "label" class2 : label
#  Association*(──>):--> example class1 "label" --> "label" class2 : label
#  Aggregation (──◇ ):--o example class1 "label" --o "label" class2 : label
#  Composition (──◆ ):--* example class1 "label" --* "label" class2 : label
# The example below shows 
# Studen ───────────── Course
#              |
# 			   |
# 		   Enrollment
# ```plantuml
# class Student {}
# class Course {}
# Student "0..*" - "1..*" Course
# (Student, Course) .. Enrollment
# class Enrollment {}
# ```
# Inheritance and realization are clear: they express vertical relationships between classes, or
# between a class and an interface. The other four express reference, horizontal relationships
# between classes or between a class and an interface, and are harder to tell apart. Many real-world
# relationships are hard to pin down precisely; as mentioned above, these relationships are
# semantic, so they cannot be fully distinguished at the code level;
# Overall, the strength of the latter relationships increases in this order: dependency <
# association < aggregation < composition; aggregation and composition are both kinds of association
snippet plantUmlClassLinkGeneralization class relationship generalization/is-a(--|> ──▷ ), inheritance, example: A 'label' --|> 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label , A extends B (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1}  --|> ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkRealization class relationship realization(..|>  --▷ ), a class implements an interface, example: A 'label' ..|> 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label , A implements B (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} ..|> ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkDependency class relationship dependency(..> -->), a weaker form of dependency, a method of one object uses another object, example: A 'label' ..> 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label , a method of A uses B (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} ..> ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkAssociationOneWay class relationship one-way association(--> ──>), uses relationship, one object uses (holds as a global or class attribute) another object (e.g. a classroom has desks), example:  A 'label' --> 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label , A has B as an attribute or global (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} --> ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkAssociationTwoWay class relationship two-way association(-- ──), mutual use, two objects use (hold as a global or class attribute) each other (e.g. a teacher has many students and each student has a teacher), example:  A 'label' -- 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label , A has B as an attribute or global and B has A (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} -- ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkAggregation class relationship aggregation/has-a(o-- ◇ ──), whole-part relationship (a special association), the part can exist independently of the whole (e.g. a company and its employees), example: A 'label' o-- 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label, A has B as an attribute or global, B aggregates into A (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} --o ${2:class2}

snippet plantUmlClassLinkComposition class relationship composition/contains-a(*-- ◆ ──), containment (a special association, strong aggregation), the part cannot exist without the whole (e.g. a person and their brain), example: A 'label' *-- 'label, B can be (B,C) for two classes B,C' B : label, A has B as an attribute or global, B composes A (replace single quotes with double quotes)
	${1:class1} --* ${2:class2}

# #Class diagram##Abstract and static
snippet plantUmlClassStatic use the {static} or {abstract} modifier to define static or abstract methods or attributes, example {static} String id
	{static}

snippet plantUmlClassAbstract use the {static} or {abstract} modifier to define static or abstract methods or attributes, example {abstract} void methods()
	{abstract}

# #Class diagram##Advanced class body
# PlantUML groups methods and attributes automatically by default; you can define separators to
# reorder methods and attributes. The following separators
# are available: -- .. == __.
# You can also put a title in a separator:
# @startuml
# class Foo1 {
# You can use
# several lines
# ..
# as you want
# and group
# ==
# things together.
# __
# You can have as many groups
# as you want
# --
# End of class
# }
# class User {
# .. Simple Getter ..
# + getName()
# + getAddress()
# .. Some setter ..
# + setName()
# __ private data __
# int age
# -- encrypted --
# String password
# }
# @enduml
# #Class diagram##Notes and stereotypes
# To change the position of a note relative to the label, use note left on link, note right on link,
# note on top, note bottom on link (placing it left, right, above or below the label respectively)
# ```plantuml
# @startuml
# class Object << general >> {
#     String name
#     + String name()
#     + String name(int length)
# }
# Object <|--- ArrayList : a link
# note on link #red: this is a note on a link, in red
# note top of Object : In java, every class\nextends this one. (note on the Object class) 
# note "This is a floating note (not attached to a class)" as N1
# note "This note is attached to\nseveral objects." as N2
# Object .. N2
# N2 .. ArrayList
# class Foo {}
# note left: defined after the class, points at Foo 
# note right of Object::name
# note on the Object.name field
# end note
# note left of Object::name()
# note on the Object.name() method
# end note
# note left of Object::"name(int length)"
# note on the Object.name(int length) method; wrap overloaded names in ""
# end note

# Foo --|> Object : a link
# note right on link #blue
# This is a note on a link, in blue; it must come after the link
# end note
# @enduml
# ```

# #Class diagram##Packages
# You can set the default style with: skinparam packageStyle, or use the matching stereotype on a
# package
# scale 750 width
# package foo1 <<Node>> {}
# package foo2 <<Rectangle>> {}
# package foo3 <<Folder>> {}
# package foo4 <<Frame>> {}
# package foo5 <<Cloud>> {}
# package foo1.foo6 <<Database>> {}
# You can also define links between packages as in the example below:
# foo1 +-- foo1.foo6
snippet plantUmlClassPackage class diagram package; classes in the same package go inside the package's {}
	package "${1:package name}" #DDDDDD {
	    $0
	}

# #Class diagram##Namespaces
# When using packages (as opposed to namespaces), the class name is the unique identifier of a
# class. This means classes in different packages cannot have the same name.
snippet plantUmlClassNamespaces class diagram namespace; use 'set namespaceSeparator .' to create namespaces automatically, namespace com.className {}. When using packages (as opposed to namespaces), the class name is the unique identifier of a class. This means classes in different packages cannot have the same name.
	namespace ${1:namespace name} #DDDDDD {
	    $0
	}

# #Class diagram##Layout helpers
# Sometimes the default layout is not perfect... you can use the together keyword to group some
# classes: the layout engine tries to keep them together (as if in a package). You can also force
# the layout with hidden links
# @startuml
# class Bar1
# class Bar2
# together {
# class Together1
# class Together2
# class Together3
# }
# Together1 - Together2
# Together2 - Together3
# Together2 -[hidden]--> Bar1
# Bar1 -[hidden]> Bar2
# @enduml
snippet plantUmlClassTogether Sometimes the default layout is not perfect... you can use the together keyword to group some classes: the layout engine tries to keep them together (as if in a package) 
	together {
	    $0
	}

# #Sequence Diagram

# ##Sequence diagram participants#Sequence diagram
# - participant (participant) if it only has a name
# - actor (actor)
# - boundary (boundary)
# - control (control)
# - entity (entity)
# - database (database)
# - collections (collections)
# - queue (queue)

# #Sequence diagram##Relationships
# | Symbol | Example                                  | Description |
# | ->     | draws a message/request between participants |         |
# | ->>    | return message                           |             |
# | --x  |

# #Sequence diagram##Example#
# ```plantuml
# @startuml
# 'as renames a participant. Note: participant is the name shown in the sequence diagram, Foo is the
# unique identifier in the code
# participant participant as Foo
# actor Foo1
# boundary boundary as Foo2
# control control as Foo3
# entity entity as Foo4
# database database as Foo5
# collections collections as Foo6
# queue queue as Foo7
# Foo -> Foo1 : To actor
# Foo -> Foo2 : To boundary
# Foo -> Foo3 : To control
# Foo -> Foo4 : To entity
# Foo -> Foo5 : To database
# Foo -> Foo6 : To collections
# Foo -> Foo7 : To queue
# Foo ->
# @enduml
# ```


snippet plantUmlSeqActivate sequence diagram with lifelines, i.e. a sequence diagram with return values
	${1:object1} -> ${2:object2}: ${3:call}
	activate $2
	$0
	$2 --> $1: ${4:return}
	deactivate $2
